#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172098 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171904} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415277}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171905} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723759} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723786}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172100 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171906} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723823} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723846}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171907} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724086} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724147}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173169 :tutorial/content "The fundamental theorem of line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is a piecewise-smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and $f$ is differentiable function such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ \n\nThis theorem tells us that the line integral of a conservative vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla f$ is determined by the values of $f$ at the endpoints $A$ and $B$ of $C$ only. The integral does *not* depend on the path between $A$ and $B.$ \n\nIn particular, if $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, then we say that $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r\n$$ is **path-independent.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "ab2cc7cb-1fa4-5e9c-92d4-551aeb945c53" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5145 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173199 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\Bbb R^2.$\nA **vector field** on $\\Bbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a *vector* $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ in $\\Bbb R^2.$\n\n![](images/5e/5ef5420c0e53b3abe5233a27c1845415d050d5bb95b7ea26b156dfcdea4e6633.png)\n\nFor example, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = (x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (x-y)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nThis vector field maps the point $(2,1)$ to the vector $3\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j},$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\mathbf{F} ( 2 , 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = ( 2 + 1 ) \\hspace{0.167em} \\mathbf{i} + ( 2 - 1 ) \\hspace{0.167em} \\mathbf{j} = 3 \\hspace{0.167em} \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nIn general, any vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ on $\\Bbb R^2$ can be expressed in terms of its component functions $P$ and $Q$ as follows:\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\nIn the example above, we had\n$$\nP(x,y) = x+y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x-y.\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can easily extend our definition of a vector field to three-dimensional or even $n$-dimensional space. For example, if $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3,$ then a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ in $\\mathbb R^3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cae9e50e-4613-539f-b9a0-1b900b237f89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5782 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173200 :tutorial/content "We can find the domain of a vector field by calculating the intersection of the domains of its component functions.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\ln\\left(4-x^2-y^2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhose component functions are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\ln\\left(4-x^2-y^2\\right), \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{x-1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf F,$ we find the domain of each component function and then compute their intersection.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n4-x^2-y^2 \\gt 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^2+y^2 \\lt 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: x^2+y^2 \\lt 4 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhich is the interior of a circle of radius $2,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/0b/0b76751de4305ed2cd3410ea52602756dc05c6d4f5c2a848e2c52220e3a445d1.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-1 \\geq 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: x \\geq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhich is the half-plane shown below:\n\n![](images/88/88dee5adf4ac85267164d42c2256c8ae25dae0ac9e74284e04186ef54c057811.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = D_1 \\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: x^2 + y^2 \\lt 4, \\, x\\geq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe domain of $\\mathbf F$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c7/c7ec35df55bcb910098888ff32cef5566c522c5d469e94409cd96e8762a63a8f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e4422ab4-0bcf-59d8-8682-06941aa354ac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5784 :tutorial/title "The Domain of a Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173207 :tutorial/content "There is some nice intuition behind the flux form of Green's theorem that makes it easier to remember.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with component functions $P$ and $Q$ defined over a region $D.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem in flux form states that the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf F$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the boundary of $D.$\n\n![](images/47/47a81125069be94b4ccda6f228ff1b8253f99595c6f3c4ae0ca710bdb796215f.png)\n\n    \n\nRecall that the divergence (i.e., the microscopic flux) of $\\mathbf F$ at a point is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nIf we sum (integrate) the divergence at every point over the entire domain $D,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y} \\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThis expression gives us one side of Green's theorem in flux form.\n\nThe macroscopic divergence is simply the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the boundary of $D{:}$\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\mathbf n \\, \\textrm d s\n$$\n\nEquating the sum of the divergences with the flux, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y} \\right)\\textrm d A = \\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\mathbf n \\, \\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nThe flux form of Green's theorem can be summarized in a single picture, as follows:\n\n![](images/0f/0f8ba4b7e853e3b59e8677b0954c3cfae1b9c482750e13a742bd4d45eda8005d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "9016229b-93aa-58fc-857c-2a2d3f337ca4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5862 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173208 :tutorial/content "Green's theorem allows us to write line integrals as double integrals, and we've seen how Green's theorem simplifies circulation calculations. Even more, Green's theorem can also be used to simplify flux calculations!\n\nTo see how, we first recall that the flux of $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) =  P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\mathbf j$ across $C$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s= \\oint\\limits_C P\\, \\textrm{d}y -Q\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is closed and $\\mathbf n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nNow, let's set $M= -Q$ and $N=P$. Then, by the circulation form of Green's theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x & ={∮}_{C}N\\,\\text{d}y+M\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}M\\,\\text{d}x+N\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂N}{∂x}-\\frac{∂M}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}-\\left(-\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right))\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we've obtained the **Green's theorem in flux form**, which we restate below:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} +\\dfrac{\\partial  Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can also write Green's theorem in flux form as\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem in flux form allows us to convert a flux line integral into an equivalent double integral." :tutorial/id #uuid "7f3b4b70-8b58-53d1-bc8d-a42daddfab4d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5868 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175838} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519842} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519833}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176023 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175878} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2eca638-7c7b-51ed-99e3-3b3156d13669" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562815} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562808}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175879} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563859} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563860}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177037 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\left( e^{-x^2}- x^2y \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left(y+xy^2\\right) \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the unit circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1,$ oriented counterclockwise. \n\nDirect evaluation of this integral is likely to be a difficult task. However, we can make light work of this tricky integral using Green's theorem.\n\nRecall that Green's theorem states the following:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ (shown below) satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/dd/dd859c753a8a5445eafab5db4f1e796b48997e1abb1728accd03fe3825e595d6.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^{-x^2}- x^2y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe obtain the partial derivatives   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{-{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis double integral will be easier to evaluate if we switch to plane polar coordinates. \n\nThe region $D$ in $xy$-coordinates can be represented as the region $\\Delta$ in polar coordinates, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral by applying the change of variables, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0b5a23c7-4ccf-5122-8b5f-98fbabb0e367" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5838 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179606} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722910} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722955}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179607} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048540}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421760}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179608} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722696} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722743}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180725 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ and $C$ be a *closed* curve in the $xy$-plane. Recall that the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nCalculating flux using this definition is often cumbersome because we need to calculate $\\mathbf n,$ the outward-pointing unit normal vector. \n\nIn practice, we often use the following alternative formula to calculate the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson.\n\n**Watch Out!** When applying this formula, we must choose a positively-oriented parameterization for $C.$ This formula will *not* work if $C$ is negatively oriented!" :tutorial/id #uuid "c74e835b-f210-5a77-8d6e-aef474c89522" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5851 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180727 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ through a piecewise-smooth, closed curve $C$ is a unique scalar number that represents the net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$ \n\nThe question now is, does the notion of flux exist for curves that are not closed?\n\nIndeed, it is possible to define the notion of flux through a non-closed curve. However, there is one crucial difference we need to bear in mind:\n\n- In the case of a smooth, closed curve, it is always clear which direction is \"out.\" Therefore, the outward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ is unique at every point on $C.$\n\n- However, on a smooth, non-closed curve, there is no clear notion of \"in\" and \"out.\" Instead, we can choose between *two* normal vectors at any point on the curve, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d1/d193eb8109ead15de50c06a7bb0b654243173d882bd361f9fd34026aa23c1b5f.png)\n\nTherefore, when computing the flux of a vector field through a non-closed curve, we first specify the direction that the unit normal points and then measure the flux relative to that direction. \n\nFor example, for the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and curve $C$ below, one choice for the unit normal will give a positive number, and the other a negative number.\n\n![](images/4f/4f92dd5334b5836703857c081049f126d39c141e84d8e94171e338a162cf3cc4.png)\n\nFinally, to calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across a non-closed curve $C,$ we can continue to use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** To apply this formula, we usually need to parameterize $C.$ It is *vital* that we pick a parameterization where the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ is a $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e2794e26-620d-59e9-85de-a3bb49f6991a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5869 :tutorial/title "Calculating Flux for Non-Closed Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180728 :tutorial/content "Let's now derive the following formula for the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that if $C$ is parameterized by the function $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ then \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{y'(t)\\,\\mathbf i - x'(t)\\,\\mathbf j}{||\\mathbf r'(t)||},\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that $C$ is positively-oriented. \n\nSubstituting our expression for $\\mathbf n$ into our line integral definition of flux and using the fact that $\\textrm d s = ||\\mathbf r'(t)|| \\,\\textrm d t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\right)||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\cancel{||{r}^{′}(t)||}}\\right)\\cancel{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot ({y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P(x(t),y(t)){y}^{′}(t)-Q(x(t),y(t)){x}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cc71a06d-4832-5949-91b4-71cb0f8185ca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5870 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Main Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198379 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198021} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa6b152-86d3-5f96-8a94-57f86a17a84d" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401261}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Over a Surface"}]}
